(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-anatomy-master-exam-form-g"]={"title":"Anatomy Exam 3 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form G","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"Within the pars nervosa, the homogenous intercellular substance that precipitates on fixation to form a distinctive structure is called a:","choices":["Negri body","Nissl body","Herring body","Barr body"],"answer":2,"exp":"On fixation, the homogenous intercellular substance in the pars nervosa precipitates to form Herring's body. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"The bundles of nerve fibers in the pars nervosa arise in the hypothalamus and descend to the posterior lobe, forming which named tract?","choices":["Spinothalamic sensory tract","Hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract","Corticospinal motor tract","Mammillothalamic fiber tract"],"answer":1,"exp":"These fibers form the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract (HHT), descending from the hypothalamus to the pars nervosa. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Each lobe of the thyroid gland measures approximately what dimensions (length x width x thickness)?","choices":["2 x 1 x 1 cm","10 x 6 x 4 cm","8 x 5 x 3 cm","5 x 3 x 2 cm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each thyroid lobe is approximately 5 x 3 x 2 cm (length x width x thickness). — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"The thyroid isthmus measures approximately what dimensions (length x width)?","choices":["1.25 x 1.25 cm","2.5 cm x 2.0 cm","0.5 cm x 0.5 cm","4.0 cm x 3.5 cm"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thyroid isthmus measures approximately 1.25 x 1.25 cm. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"An occasional conical pyramidal lobe of the thyroid, usually present toward the left, connects to the hyoid bone via a fibrous or fibromuscular cord known as the:","choices":["Suspensory ligament of Berry","Sternothyroid muscle band","Levator glandulae thyroideae","Thyrohyoid membrane fibers"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pyramidal lobe connects to the hyoid bone via the levator glandulae thyroideae, a fibrous or fibromuscular cord. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"The apex of each thyroid lobe lies at the level of which laryngeal landmark?","choices":["The lower border of the cricoid cartilage","The oblique line of the thyroid cartilage","The greater cornu of the hyoid bone","The apex of the arytenoid cartilage"],"answer":1,"exp":"The apex of each thyroid lobe is at the level of the oblique line of the thyroid cartilage of the larynx. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"The base of each thyroid lobe lies at the level of which tracheal ring(s)?","choices":["1st or 2nd","6th or 7th","2nd or 3rd","4th or 5th"],"answer":3,"exp":"The base of each thyroid lobe lies at the level of the 4th or 5th tracheal ring. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"The thyroid gland spans which cervical-to-thoracic vertebral levels in the lower neck?","choices":["C5 to T1","T2 to T4","C1 to C3","C3 to C5"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thyroid gland lies in the lower part of the neck at the level of the 5th cervical (C5) to the 1st thoracic (T1) vertebrae. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"The superior and middle thyroid veins usually drain into which vein?","choices":["Subclavian vein","Internal jugular vein","External jugular vein","Brachiocephalic vein directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior and middle thyroid veins usually end in the internal jugular vein. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Lymphatic drainage of the thyroid gland is directed to paratracheal nodes and to deep cervical nodes located inferior to which muscle?","choices":["Thyrohyoid","Omohyoid","Sternocleidomastoid","Digastric"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thyroid lymphatics drain to deep cervical nodes inferior to the omohyoid muscle, along the internal jugular vein. — Endocrine for Posting.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Lymphoid tissue on the posterior surface of the tongue is known as the:","choices":["Lingual tonsils","Tubal tonsils","Pharyngeal tonsil","Palatine tonsils"],"answer":0,"exp":"The posterior surface of the tongue contains the lingual tonsils. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"The esophagus begins at approximately which cervical vertebral level, corresponding to the upper esophageal sphincter?","choices":["C6","C8","C2","C4"],"answer":0,"exp":"The esophagus begins at approximately the C6 level, where the upper esophageal sphincter is located. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus at approximately which vertebral level?","choices":["T10","T8","T12","T6"],"answer":0,"exp":"The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus at approximately the T10 level. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"The esophagus terminates at approximately which vertebral level, where it meets the stomach at the cardiac sphincter?","choices":["T10","T8","T11","T9"],"answer":2,"exp":"The esophagus terminates at approximately the T11 level, at the cardiac (inferior esophageal) sphincter. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"The three named portions of the esophagus, in order from superior to inferior, are the:","choices":["Pharyngeal, cervical, abdominal","Cervical, thoracic, pelvic","Cervical, diaphragmatic, abdominal","Cervical, mediastinal, abdominal"],"answer":3,"exp":"The esophagus is divided into cervical, mediastinal, and abdominal portions as it descends toward the stomach. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"The cricopharyngeal muscle forms which sphincter of the esophagus?","choices":["The inferior (cardiac) sphincter","The pyloric sphincter of the stomach","The ileocecal valve sphincter","The superior esophageal sphincter"],"answer":3,"exp":"The superior esophageal sphincter, located where the pharynx and esophagus meet, is formed by the cricopharyngeal muscle. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"The adult esophagus measures approximately how long as it travels from the pharynx to the stomach?","choices":["10 cm","40 cm","15 cm","25 cm"],"answer":3,"exp":"The esophagus is approximately 25 cm long in the adult, extending from the pharynx to the stomach. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"The submucosa of the esophagus is described as containing thick elastic fibers and which glandular structures?","choices":["Mucous glands","Gastric pits","Crypts of Lieberkühn","Brunner's glands"],"answer":0,"exp":"The esophageal submucosa contains thick elastic fibers and mucous glands. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which stomach region is the most superior and lies under the left dome of the diaphragm?","choices":["Cardia","Pyloric antrum","Body","Fundus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The fundus is the most superior region of the stomach. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which mucosal specialization of the stomach interior consists of a narrow channel along the lesser curvature that helps direct fluids toward the pylorus?","choices":["Gastric pits","Gastric canal","Rugae","Plicae circulares"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gastric canal is the mucosal specialization of the stomach interior described alongside rugae, pits, and glands. — GI I & II for posting.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"The suspensory ligament of the ovary connects the ovary to which structure?","choices":["The uterus","The pelvic wall","The rectum","The urinary bladder"],"answer":1,"exp":"The suspensory ligament of the ovary extends from the ovary to the pelvic wall. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"The Müllerian (paramesonephric) ducts arise from the coelomic epithelium on the lateral aspect of the mesonephros at approximately which week of development?","choices":["12th week","20th week","6th week","3rd week"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Müllerian ducts arise from coelomic epithelium around the 6th week of development. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"The Müllerian ducts develop into the female reproductive organs specifically in the absence of which hormone?","choices":["Testosterone from the fetal testes","Anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH)","Estradiol from ovarian tissue","Progesterone from the placenta"],"answer":1,"exp":"In the absence of anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH), the Müllerian ducts persist and develop into the major internal female reproductive organs. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"The caudal portions of the Müllerian ducts fuse in the midline to form the:","choices":["Urogenital sinus cavity","Uterovaginal primordium","Gubernaculum ligament","Mesonephric duct remnant"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fused caudal portions of the Müllerian ducts form the uterovaginal primordium. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"The upper one-third of the vagina develops from the fused Müllerian ducts, while the lower vagina develops from the:","choices":["Yolk sac","Allantois","Mesonephric duct","Urogenital sinus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lower vagina develops from the urogenital sinus, while the upper vagina develops from the fused Müllerian ducts. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Venous drainage of the right ovary empties into which vessel?","choices":["The internal iliac vein","The inferior vena cava","The external iliac vein","The left renal vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"The right ovary drains venously into the inferior vena cava, while the left ovary drains into the left renal vein. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Venous drainage of the left ovary empties into which vessel?","choices":["The left renal vein","The splenic vein","The internal iliac vein","The inferior vena cava directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"The left ovary drains into the left renal vein, while the right ovary drains directly into the inferior vena cava. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Venous drainage of the ovaries occurs via which named venous network?","choices":["Vesical venous plexus","Pampiniform plexus","Presacral venous plexus","Uterine venous plexus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ovarian veins drain via the pampiniform plexus before continuing to the IVC (right) or left renal vein (left). — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Lymphatics of the ovaries follow the ovarian blood vessels to ascend to which lymph nodes?","choices":["Axillary lymph nodes","Lumbar (para-aortic) lymph nodes","Superficial inguinal lymph nodes","Deep cervical lymph nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Ovarian lymphatics follow the ovarian blood vessels to ascend to the lumbar lymph nodes. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Parasympathetic innervation of the ovary arises from which nerves?","choices":["Pudendal nerve","Greater splanchnic nerve","Vagus nerve fibers","Pelvic splanchnic nerves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Parasympathetic innervation of the ovary arises from the pelvic splanchnic nerves. — Female Reproductive Tract for lecure.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"The thickened tissue extending from the anus, across the scrotum, and along the anterior surface of the penis, marking the division between the two scrotal chambers, is the:","choices":["Internal scrotal septum","Raphe of the scrotum","The full spermatic cord","The dartos muscle fascia"],"answer":1,"exp":"The raphe of the scrotum is the visible thickened tissue marking the division between the two scrotal chambers. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"The tunica vaginalis lining each scrotal cavity is best classified as which type of tissue?","choices":["An erectile tissue","A serous membrane","A skeletal muscle","A fibrous capsule"],"answer":1,"exp":"The tunica vaginalis is a serous membrane that lines each scrotal cavity and reduces friction between its layers. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"The fibrous, connective-tissue lining of the testis that extends inward to form septa is the:","choices":["Mediastinum testis","Tunica albuginea","Tunica vaginalis","Rete testis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The tunica albuginea is the fibrous connective-tissue lining of the testis that extends inward to form septa. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"The tunica vaginalis of the testis is continuous with the surrounding tissue of which structure?","choices":["The prostate","The seminal gland","The epididymis","The ductus deferens"],"answer":2,"exp":"The tunica vaginalis is continuous with the surrounding tissue of the epididymis. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Nurse cells of the seminiferous tubules are also known by which other name?","choices":["Interstitial cells","Chief cells","Sertoli cells","Leydig cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Nurse cells are also known as Sertoli cells. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"As seminiferous tubules uncoil in the mediastinum area, they lead into which structures?","choices":["Rete testis","Ductus deferens","Efferent ductules","Straight tubules"],"answer":3,"exp":"Seminiferous tubules uncoil to lead into the straight tubules within the mediastinum. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"The straight tubules interconnect with each other within the mediastinum to form which structure?","choices":["The epididymal head","The rete testis","The ejaculatory duct","The efferent ductules"],"answer":1,"exp":"The straight tubules interconnect to form the rete testis within the mediastinum. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"According to the lecture's histology figure, the tail of a mature sperm cell measures approximately:","choices":["55 µm","100 µm","5 µm","20 µm"],"answer":0,"exp":"The tail of the sperm is depicted as approximately 55 µm long. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"According to the lecture's histology figure, the head of a mature sperm cell measures approximately:","choices":["25 µm","1 µm","5 µm","10 µm"],"answer":2,"exp":"The head of the sperm is depicted as approximately 5 µm. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"According to the lecture's histology figure, the middle piece of a mature sperm cell measures approximately:","choices":["15 µm","1 µm","30 µm","5 µm"],"answer":3,"exp":"The middle piece of the sperm is depicted as approximately 5 µm. — HUGHIE Male Reproductive System summer 2026-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Arterial blood destined for the glomerulus travels: aorta, renal artery, segmental arteries, lobar arteries, interlobar arteries, arcuate artery, interlobular artery, then which vessel?","choices":["Vasa recta","Peritubular capillary","Efferent arteriole","Afferent arteriole"],"answer":3,"exp":"After the interlobular artery, blood travels through the afferent arteriole before reaching the glomerulus. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Venous blood draining the kidney travels: vasa recta, interlobular vein, arcuate vein, interlobar vein, then which vessel, before the IVC?","choices":["Gonadal vein","Renal vein","Segmental vein","Lobar vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"After the interlobar vein, blood drains into the renal vein before reaching the inferior vena cava. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"The efferent arterioles branch into peritubular capillaries or which other capillary network?","choices":["Arcuate capillaries","Vasa recta","Interlobular capillaries","Glomerular capillaries"],"answer":1,"exp":"The efferent arterioles branch into either peritubular capillaries or the vasa recta. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"The renal plexus, which innervates the kidney, accompanies which vessel and enters through the hilum?","choices":["The ureter","The gonadal artery","The renal vein","The renal artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"The renal plexus accompanies the renal artery and enters the kidney through the hilum. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Greater splanchnic nerve fibers to the kidney arise from which spinal nerve roots?","choices":["T10–T11","T5–T9","T1–T4","T12 only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The greater splanchnic nerves supplying the kidney arise from T5–T9 nerve roots. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Lesser splanchnic nerve fibers to the kidney arise from which spinal nerve roots?","choices":["T12","T5–T9","L1–L2","T10–T11"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lesser splanchnic nerves supplying the kidney arise from T10–T11 nerve roots. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"The least splanchnic nerve, arising from the T12 root, is described as serving mainly which function for the kidney?","choices":["Sensory innervation","Innervation of the adrenal medulla only","Motor innervation of the ureter","Parasympathetic secretomotor function"],"answer":0,"exp":"The least splanchnic nerve (T12) is described as mainly sensory for the kidney. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"According to the lecture's receptor terminology, 'adrenergic' receptors correspond to which division of the autonomic nervous system?","choices":["Parasympathetic","Somatic","Sympathetic","Enteric"],"answer":2,"exp":"Adrenergic receptors correspond to the sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"According to the lecture's receptor terminology, 'cholinergic' and 'muscarinic' receptors correspond to which division of the autonomic nervous system?","choices":["Somatic motor","None (they are unrelated to the ANS)","Parasympathetic","Sympathetic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cholinergic and muscarinic receptors correspond to the parasympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"The renal corpuscle is composed of the glomerulus and which capsule?","choices":["Bowman's (glomerular) capsule","The renal capsule itself","The adipose capsule layer","The perinephric capsule"],"answer":0,"exp":"The renal corpuscle is composed of the glomerulus and the glomerular (Bowman's) capsule. — 18. NephrologyUrinary System - 2026.pptx, Slide 29"}]};
